Clark's method is not used to remove permanent hardness of water. This method is used to remove temporary hardness of water.

\begin{array}{r}
\mathrm{Ca}\left(\mathrm{HCO}_3\right)_2+\mathrm{Ca}(\mathrm{OH})_2 \longrightarrow 2 \mathrm{CaCO}_3 \downarrow+2 \mathrm{H}_2 \mathrm{O} \\
\mathrm{Mg}\left(\mathrm{HCO}_3\right)_2+2 \mathrm{Ca}(\mathrm{OH})_2 \longrightarrow \\
2 \mathrm{CaCO}_3 \downarrow+\mathrm{Mg}(\mathrm{OH})_2 \downarrow+2 \mathrm{H}_2 \mathrm{O}
\end{array}

If excess of lime is added, water will again become hard because of adsorption of $\mathrm{CO}_2$ from the atmosphere.

\mathrm{Ca}(\mathrm{OH})_2+2 \mathrm{CO}_2 \longrightarrow \mathrm{Ca}\left(\mathrm{HCO}_3\right)_2

The temporary hardness of water is due to the presence of bicarbonates of $\mathrm{Ca}$ and $\mathrm{Mg}$. This may be removed by Clark's process. In this process, addition of calculated quantity of lime causes precipitation of $\mathrm{CaCO}_3$ as

\begin{array}{r}
\mathrm{Ca}\left(\mathrm{HCO}_3\right)_2+\mathrm{Ca}(\mathrm{OH})_2 \longrightarrow 2 \mathrm{CaCO}_3 \downarrow+2 \mathrm{H}_2 \mathrm{O} \\
\text { and } \mathrm{Mg}\left(\mathrm{HCO}_3\right)_2+2 \mathrm{Ca}(\mathrm{OH})_2 \underset{2}{\longrightarrow} 2 \mathrm{CaCO}_3 \downarrow \\
+\mathrm{Mg}(\mathrm{OH})_2+2 \mathrm{H}_2 \mathrm{O}
\end{array}

